<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

    <!--
      spring-boot-devtools is the Maven library. Dependency-Check's CPE Analyzer also
      maps it onto two VMware *IDE tooling* products that merely share the name:

        cpe:/a:vmware:spring_tools       - Spring Tools 4 for Eclipse
        cpe:/a:vmware:spring_boot_tools  - the "Spring Boot Tools" VSCode extension

      Those are editors, not this library, so every CVE filed against them is a false
      positive here. The mismatch bites because the version ranges overlap by
      coincidence: devtools 4.1.1 falls under "Spring Tools for Eclipse < 4.16.1".

      Confirmed against the NVD record for CVE-2022-31691 (CVSS 9.8), which describes
      SnakeYAML-based RCE in Spring Tools 4 for Eclipse and VSCode extensions, and
      lists only IDE/editor CPEs as affected:
        https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2022-31691

      Independently, spring-boot-devtools is declared <optional>true</optional> and is
      stripped from the repackaged jar by spring-boot-maven-plugin, so it is absent
      from the container image and cannot reach production at all.

      Deliberately NOT suppressed: cpe:/a:vmware:spring_boot. That mapping is
      legitimate for a Spring Boot artifact, so genuine Spring Boot CVEs still gate
      the build through it.
    -->
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
            spring-boot-devtools is not the Spring Tools IDE or the Spring Boot Tools
            VSCode extension. Suppresses the IDE-tooling CPEs only, not vmware:spring_boot.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.springframework\.boot/spring-boot-devtools@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cpe>cpe:/a:vmware:spring_tools</cpe>
        <cpe>cpe:/a:vmware:spring_boot_tools</cpe>
    </suppress>

</suppressions>
